                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  H
                  \&amp;\overset{\sim}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                  H^\ast
                  \&amp;\overset{\sim}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                  H
                  \\\
                  \\vert \psi \rangle
                  \&amp;\mapsto&amp;
                  \\langle \psi \vert
                  \&amp;\mapsto&amp;
                  \\vert \psi \rangle
                  }
          %p
            are now complex
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-linear+map"} anti-linear
            and hence not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+spaces"} complex vector spaces
          %p
            (What one does get is a complex-linear isomorphism to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-dual+linear+space"} anti-dual linear space
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#KaroubiVillamayor73"} Karoubi &amp; Villamayor 1973, p. 58 (3 of 31)
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#Karoubi10"} Karoubi 2010, §1
          %p
            But one may absorb this anti-linearity into an ambient category so that Hermitian/unitary structure regarded
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internally
            to that category looks just like Euclidean/orthogonal structure. Since this uses basic structures known from Atiyah’s
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Real+K-theory"} Real K-theory
            — with capital “R”,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KR-theory"} KR-theory
            — it is suggestive and convenient to refer to “Real” structures, as follows:
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p Write
            #eq:CategoryOfRealvectorBundlesWithInvolution.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_{\mathbb{R}}^{\mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2}
                    \\;\;\;
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\;\;\;
                    Func\big(
                    \\mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2
                    ,\,
                    Mod_{\mathbb{R}}
                    \\big)
            %p
              for the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+spaces"} real vector spaces
              equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
              (equivalently the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping+groupoid"} delooping groupoid
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group+of+order+two"} cyclic group of order two
              to the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_{\mathbb{R}}
              of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+spaces"} real vector spaces
            %p
              This becomes a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              whose modoidal stucture
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
              equipped with the tensor product of their involutions.
          #RealComplexNumbers.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (Real complex numbers)
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
              regarded as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+space"} real vector space
              equipped with its real-linear involution given by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
              is an object of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CategoryOfRealvectorBundlesWithInvolution"} (4)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+homomorphism"} algebra homomorphism
              on the complex numbers, the usual product operation makes this a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CategoryOfRealvectorBundlesWithInvolution"} (4)
              = succeed ":" do
                %em Real complex numbers
            #eq:RealComplexNumbersObject.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (5)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ↷
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Mon
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{Z}_2
                    \\curvearrowright
                    \\mathbb{C}
                    \\;\in\;
                    Mon\big(
                    Mod_{\mathbb{R}}^{\mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2}
                    \\big)
                    \\,.
            %p
          %p
          #CategoryOfRealComplexModules.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (Real complex modules)
              We say that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+objects"} module objects
              over the Real complex numbers
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RealComplexNumbersObject"} (5)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              to
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CategoryOfRealvectorBundlesWithInvolution"} (4)
              is the category of
              = succeed ":" do
                %em Real complex modules
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ↷
                        %mi ℂ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≡
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ↷
                        %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_{
                    \\mathbb{Z}_2 \curvearrowright \mathbb{C}
                    }
                    \\;\;\;\;
                    \\equiv
                    \\;\;\;\;
                    Mod_{
                    \\mathbb{Z}_2 \curvearrowright \mathbb{C}
                    }
                    \\big(
                    Mod_{\mathbb{R}}^{\mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2}
                    \\big)
                    \\,.
            %p
          #AntilinearInvolutionFromRealComplexModules.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              The category of Real complex modules (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryOfRealComplexModules"}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+spaces"} complex vector spaces
              equipped with
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-linear+map"} anti-linear
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involutions"} involutions
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ↷
                        %mi ℂ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi σ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi V
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mi antilinear
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_{
                    \\mathbb{Z}_2 \curvearrowright \mathbb{C}
                    }
                    \\;\;
                    \\simeq
                    \\;\;
                    \\big(
                    V \,\colon\, Mod_{\mathbb{C}}
                    \\big)
                    \\times
                    \\big(
                    \\sigma
                    \\,\colon\,
                    V
                    \\xrightarrow{
                    \\mathbb{C} antilinear
                    }
                    V
                    \\big)
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p By direct unwinding of the definitions, one finds that the module property enforces the anti-linearity of the involution:
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/AntilinInvViaRealComplexModules-230905.jpg", :width => "300px"}/
            %p
              Moreover, to see that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+products"} tensor products
              agree one can argue that the relevant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizers"} coequalizers
              (see
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+1-2#TensorProductOfModulesOverCommutativeMonoidObject"} here
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_{\mathbb{R}}^{\mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}}
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
              which
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits+of+presheaves+are+computed+objectwise"} are computed objectwise
              — hence here over the single object of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+spaces"} complex vector spaces
          %p
            The following construction is closely related to what is known as the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperbolic+functor"} hyperbolic functor
            with
            = succeed "-equivariance," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KR-theory"} KR-theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Hermitian+K-theory"} topological Hermitian K-theory
            (see the references
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesTopologicalHermitianKTheory"} there
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Recall the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+spaces"} real vector spaces
            and
            %a{:href => "real+vector+bundle#InKRTheory"} Real vector bundles
            over the point, given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexification"} complexification
            equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
            by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %br/
          #eq:RModulesAsRealBundlesViaComplexification.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (6)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -75px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/RModulesAsRealBundles-231110.jpg", :width => "520px"}/
          %p
          #HermitianFormsAreRealInnerProducts.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Hermitian forms are Real inner products)
              %br/
              Under the equivalence
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RModulesAsRealBundlesViaComplexification"} (6)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+space"} real
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product+space"} inner product space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{V} \,\in\, Mod_{\mathbb{R}}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mrow
                          %mi ℝ
                    %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \,\colon\, \mathscr{V} \otimes_{{}_{\mathbb{R}}} \mathscr{V} \to \mathbb{R}
              equipped with an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometric
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+structure"} complex structure
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                      %mo →
                      %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \,\colon\, \mathscr{V} \to \mathscr{V}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\big(J(-), J(-)\big) = g(-,-)
              is sent to corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian form
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ≡
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %mi J
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -\vert-\rangle \,\equiv\, g(-,-) + \mathrm{i}g\big(J(-),-\big)
            %p
              %br/
            %p
              %br/
            #eq:RealInnerProductsAsHermitianForms.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (7)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -75px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/RealInnerProductsAsHermitianForms-231110b.jpg", :width => "530px"}/
            %p
          Here
          %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
            %semantics
              %mrow
                %msub
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                  %mrow
                    %mo ±
                    %mi J
              %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{V}_{\pm J}
          denotes the complex vector space whose underlying real vector space is
          %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
            %semantics
              %mrow
                %mi.mathscript 𝒱
              %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{V}
          and with complex structure
          = succeed "." do
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ±
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm J
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              On the right we used the following
              = succeed "-module" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ↷
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2 \curvearrowright \mathbb{C}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              from the plain complexification in
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RModulesAsRealBundlesViaComplexification"} (6)
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ComplexificationOfComplexStructure-231111.jpg", :width => "510px"}/
            %p
              which serves to bring out the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenspaces"} eigenspaces
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mi ℝ
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \otimes_{{}_\mathbb{R}} \mathbb{C}
            %p
              %br/
            %p
              %br/
            #eq:ComplexifiedComplexStructure.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (8)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -75px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ComplexifiedComplexStructure-231110.jpg", :width => "320px"}/
            %p
              With this, the isometry condition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi J
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ (J \otimes J) = g
              — which means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              factors through the
              = succeed "-eigenspace" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (+1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mrow
                          %mi ℝ
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \!\otimes_{{}_\mathbb{R}}\! J
              — implies by functoriality of the equivalence
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RModulesAsRealBundlesViaComplexification"} (6)
              that the pairing on the right of
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RealInnerProductsAsHermitianForms"} (7)
              factors through the
              = succeed "-eigenspace" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (+1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} I
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{I} \otimes \mathrm{I}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ComplexifiedComplexStructure"} (8)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                          %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{V}_{+J}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RealInnerProductsAsHermitianForms"} (7)
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/RealInnerProductProducesTradFormula-231110.jpg", :width => "880px"}/
            %p
          %p
          #HermtianSpacesAsRealComplexModules.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (Complex Hermitian spaces as Euclidean Real complex modules)
              %br/
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H, \langle-\vert-\rangle_H
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product+space"} inner product space
              (assumed non-degenerate), its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+dual+space"} linear dual space
              carries the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-linear+map"} anti-linear
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
              induced
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:AntiLinearIsomorphismWithDualVectorSpace"} (3)
              by the Hermitian form:
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HermitianSpaceAsRealComplexModule-230905.jpg", :width => "270px"}/
            %p
              Therefore the Hermitian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              induces a Real complex module by Lem.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AntilinearInvolutionFromRealComplexModules"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ↷
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ↷
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathscr{H}
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\mathbb{Z}_2 \curvearrowright
                    \\big(
                    H  \oplus H^\ast
                    \\big)
                    \\;\;
                    \\in
                    \\;\;
                    Mod_{
                    \\mathbb{Z}_2 \curvearrowright \mathbb{C}
                    \\,.
                    }
            %p
              Notice that the Real complex modules
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
              arising this way have special properties:
            %ol
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+object"} self-dual object
                = succeed "-)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coevaluation+map"} co
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation+maps"} evaluation maps
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SelfDualStructureOnRealComplexModule-230905.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
            %ol
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
                carries an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+structure"} complex structure
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
                (now of Real complex modules!) which squares to minus the
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity morphism
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ComplexStructureOnRealComplexModule-230905.jpg", :width => "300px"}/
            %p
              Due to this complex and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+object"} self-dual
              structure we may think of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
              as being a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean
              (instead of Hermitian) complex linear space but now
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
              Real complex modules.
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
              of (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"}> finite-dimensional
              , non-degenerate) complex Hermitian spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H, \langle-\vert-\rangle_H
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K, \langle-\vert-\rangle_K
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
              between them are in bijection to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
              between the corresponding Real complex modules according to Exp.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HermtianSpacesAsRealComplexModules"}
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the ordinary
                  = succeed "-complex" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{i}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                        %mo →
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \to K
                  correspond to the
                  = succeed "-complex" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                          %mi β
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{i}\beta
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi.mathscript ℋ
                        %mo →
                        %mi.mathscript 𝒦
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} \to \mathscr{K}
              %li
                %p
                  the ordinary
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+maps"} unitary maps
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                        %mo →
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \to K
                  correspond to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+maps"} orthogonal maps
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi.mathscript ℋ
                        %mo →
                        %mi.mathscript 𝒦
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} \to \mathscr{K}
                  (namely those which respect the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation+maps"} evaluation maps
                  on these
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+objects"} self-dual objects
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p This follows by straightforward unwinding of the definitions:
            %p
              First, for a homomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo →
                    %mi.mathscript 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} \to \mathscr{K}
              to commute with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{i}\beta
              its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
              complex linear map clearly needs to respect the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mo *
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msup
                        %mi K
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \oplus H^\ast \to K \oplus K^\ast
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi H
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \,\colon\,H \to K
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mo *
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi K
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^\ast \to K^\ast
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+operator"} operator adjoint
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi g
                        %mo †
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^\dagger
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ComplexLinMapsAsRealComplexHoms-230905.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
            %p Second: A unitary map is a complex linear map that preserves the Hermitian form. But with the first point above one sees that this is equivalent to preserving the evaluation map on the corresponding Real complex modules:
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/UnitaryCLinMapIsOrthRealComplexMap-230905.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
            %p
          %p
            In
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong summary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
            as Real complex modules, complex Hermitian/unitary space look like complex Euclidean/orthogonal spaces:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HermitianSpAsEuclidRealComplexMods-230906.jpg", :width => "650px"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HermitianSpacesAsEquivariantModules-230901.jpg", :width => "780"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %p (…)
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+form"}> bilinear form
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+form"} quadratic form
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sesquilinear+form"} sesquilinear form
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"}> symplectic form
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+form"} Kähler form
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hermitian+matrix"} hermitian matrix
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+manifold"} Hermitian manifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+form"} quadratic form
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bra-ket"} bra-ket
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+K-theory"} Hermitian K-theory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Hermitian forms are named in honor of the discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+forms"} quadratic forms
            due to:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Hermite"}> Charles Hermite
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sur la théorie des formes quadratiques. Premier mémoire
              %strong 47
              (1854) 313-342 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1854.47.343"}> doi:10.1515/crll.1854.47.343
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p maybe starting with
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luigi+Bianchi"}> Luigi Bianchi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Forme definite di Hermite
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sui gruppi di sostituzioni lineari con coefficienti appartenenti a corpi quadratici immaginarî
              %strong 40
              (1892) 332–412 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01443558"}> doi:10.1007/BF01443558
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p see:
          %ul
            %li
              Jürgen Elstrodt, Fritz Grunewald, Jens Mennicke:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Integral Binary Hermitian Forms
              = succeed "," do
                %em Groups Acting on Hyperbolic Space
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-03626-6_9"} doi:10.1007/978-3-662-03626-6_9
          %p
            Discussion of Hermitian forms over the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            (in laying of mathematical foundations of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %ul
            %li#vonNeumann30
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"}> John von Neumann
                , p. 64 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Allgemeine Eigenwerttheorie Hermitescher Funktionaloperatoren
                %strong 102
                (1930) 49–131 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01782338"}> doi:10.1007/BF01782338
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"}> John von Neumann
                \:
              %p
                p. 21 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematische Grundlagen der Quantenmechanik
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-642-96048-2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-96048-2
              %p
                pp. 38 in:
                %em Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Mechanics
                Princeton University Press (1955) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400889921"}> doi:10.1515/9781400889921
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_Foundations_of_Quantum_Mechanics"} Wikipedia entry
          %p Textbook account:
          %ul
            %li#Bourbaki81
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolas+Bourbaki"}> Nicolas Bourbaki
                , §V.1.1 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topological Vector Spaces
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61715-7"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-61715-7
            %li#Huybrechts04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Huybrechts"}> Daniel Huybrechts
                , §1.2 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Complex geometry: An introduction
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/b137952"} doi:10.1007/b137952
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.im.ufrj.br/~andrew/ensino/VC20-1/Huybrechts-Complex_Geometry.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Textbook accounts in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebras"} operator algebras
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+V.+Kadison"}> Richard V. Kadison
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+R.+Ringrose"} John R. Ringrose
                %em Fundamentals of the theory of operator algebras
                Vol I
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Elementary Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 15
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/gsm-15"} ISBN:978-0-8218-0819-1
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Blackadar"}> Bruce Blackadar
                , §I.1.1 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Operator Algebras – Theory of
                    = succeed "-Algebras" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mo *
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
                = succeed "," do
